const ABORTED = Symbol("aborted");

export async function* takeUntil<T>(
  source: AsyncIterable<T>,
  signal: AbortSignal,
): AsyncGenerator<T, void, undefined> {
  if (signal.aborted) return;

  const iterator = source[Symbol.asyncIterator]();
  let onAbort: (() => void) | undefined;
  const aborted = new Promise<typeof ABORTED>((resolve) => {
    onAbort = () => resolve(ABORTED);
    signal.addEventListener("abort", onAbort, { once: true });
  });

  try {
    for (;;) {
      const settled = await Promise.race([iterator.next(), aborted]);
      if (settled === ABORTED) {
        await iterator.return?.(undefined);
        return;
      }
      if (settled.done === true) return;
      yield settled.value;
    }
  } finally {
    if (onAbort) signal.removeEventListener("abort", onAbort);
  }
}

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

The pattern is a pool of workers over a shared cursor, not a chunked loop.
Chunking — take five, `Promise.all`, take the next five — is easier to write
and strictly worse: the whole chunk waits for its slowest member, so a pool of
five with one slow task runs at a concurrency of one until it finishes.

## Hint 2

A worker is a loop that takes the next index and stops when there are none
left:

```ts
let next = 0;
const worker = async () => {
  while (next < items.length) {
    const index = next++;
    results[index] = await fn(items[index] as T, index, signal);
  }
};
await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker));
```

Two things are missing from that sketch: the abort check inside the loop, and
the rejection behaviour, which `Promise.all` gives you for free — including the
part where the other workers keep running until they notice.

## Hint 3

`next++` inside an `async` function is safe because JavaScript has one thread
and no `await` sits between the read and the write. Convince yourself of that
before you reach for a lock; it is the reason this pattern is five lines rather
than fifty.

Left to you: where the abort check goes. At the top of each worker iteration is
correct and sufficient. Deciding whether to also race the abort against the
whole thing changes the timing of the rejection — decide which the prompt asks
for.

