## ex1 — build order, and the cycle that stops it

Edit `ex1/solution.ts`. The first thing `tsc --build` does with your graph.

```ts
export interface Project {
  path: string;
  references: readonly string[];
}

export type BuildOrder =
  | { ok: true; order: string[] }
  | { ok: false; cycle: string[] };

export function buildOrder(projects: readonly Project[]): BuildOrder;
```

Rules:

- dependencies come before the projects that reference them
- ties are broken by path, ascending, so the output is deterministic across
  machines
- a reference to a project that is not in the list throws
  `Error('unknown reference: <ref> from <path>')`
- a cycle returns `{ ok: false, cycle }`, where `cycle` starts and ends with the
  same project and lists the path between them, taking the
  alphabetically-first entry point into the cycle

Examples:

```ts
buildOrder([
  { path: "app", references: ["core", "ui"] },
  { path: "ui", references: ["core"] },
  { path: "core", references: [] },
])
// { ok: true, order: ["core", "ui", "app"] }

buildOrder([
  { path: "a", references: ["b"] },
  { path: "b", references: ["a"] },
])
// { ok: false, cycle: ["a", "b", "a"] }
```

```
uv run dojo test typescript v3tc05_project_references ex1
```

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

Depth-first with three colours — unvisited, in progress, done — gives you the
order and the cycle from one traversal. Kahn's algorithm gives you the order
and tells you only that *a* cycle exists, which is not enough to print a useful
message.

## Hint 2

The colour states are what detect the cycle, and the stack is what reports it:

```ts
const state = new Map<string, "open" | "closed">();
const stack: string[] = [];

const visit = (path: string): string[] | null => {
  if (state.get(path) === "closed") return null;
  if (state.get(path) === "open") return [...stack.slice(stack.indexOf(path)), path];
  // ...
};
```

Reaching a node that is still `open` means you have walked back into the path
you are currently on, and `stack.indexOf` finds where that path began.

## Hint 3

Determinism comes from two sorts, not one. Sort the entry points before you
start visiting, and sort each node's references before you recurse. Skip either
and the output depends on the order the caller happened to build the array in.

Left to you: whether to sort references in place or on a copy. Mutating an
argument's nested array is the kind of thing that works today and produces a
bug report in six months.

references.solution.ts:
// v3tc05_project_references / ex1 - reference solution (author only).

export interface Project {
  path: string;
  references: readonly string[];
}

export type BuildOrder =
  | { ok: true; order: string[] }
  | { ok: false; cycle: string[] };

export function buildOrder(projects: readonly Project[]): BuildOrder {
  const byPath = new Map(projects.map((project) => [project.path, project]));
  const state = new Map<string, "open" | "closed">();
  const stack: string[] = [];
  const order: string[] = [];

  const visit = (path: string): string[] | null => {
    if (state.get(path) === "closed") return null;
    if (state.get(path) === "open") {
      return [...stack.slice(stack.indexOf(path)), path];
    }

    const project = byPath.get(path);
    if (project === undefined) throw new Error(`unknown reference: ${path}`);

    state.set(path, "open");
    stack.push(path);
    for (const reference of [...project.references].sort()) {
      if (!byPath.has(reference)) {
        throw new Error(`unknown reference: ${reference} from ${path}`);
      }
      const cycle = visit(reference);
      if (cycle !== null) return cycle;
    }
    stack.pop();
    state.set(path, "closed");
    order.push(path);
    return null;
  };

  for (const path of projects.map((p) => p.path).sort()) {
    const cycle = visit(path);
    if (cycle !== null) return { ok: false, cycle };
  }
  return { ok: true, order };
}

// v3tc05_project_references / ex2 - reference solution (author only).

export interface Project {
  path: string;
  references: readonly string[];
}

export function affected(
  projects: readonly Project[],
  changed: readonly string[],
): string[] {
  const known = new Set(projects.map((project) => project.path));
  const dependents = new Map<string, string[]>();
  for (const project of projects) {
    for (const reference of project.references) {
      const list = dependents.get(reference);
      if (list === undefined) dependents.set(reference, [project.path]);
      else list.push(project.path);
    }
  }

  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const queue = changed.filter((path) => known.has(path));
  for (const path of queue) seen.add(path);

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const current = queue.shift() as string;
    for (const dependent of dependents.get(current) ?? []) {
      if (seen.has(dependent)) continue;
      seen.add(dependent);
      queue.push(dependent);
    }
  }

  return [...seen].sort();
}

exercises.prompt:
## ex3 — the up-to-date check

Edit `ex3/solution.ts`. This is the function that makes `tsc --build` fast, and
knowing its exact order is how you diagnose a build that rebuilds everything
every time.

```ts
export interface Project {
  path: string;
  references: readonly string[];
}

export interface BuildInfo {
  outputs: readonly string[];
  builtAt: number;
}

export interface Snapshot {
  fileTimes: Readonly<Record<string, number>>;
  buildInfos: Readonly<Record<string, BuildInfo>>;
}

export type Status =
  | "missing-buildinfo"
  | "missing-output"
  | "stale-input"
  | "stale-reference"
  | "up-to-date";

export function statusOf(
  project: Project,
  inputs: readonly string[],
  snapshot: Snapshot,
): Status;
```

Checks, in **this order**, returning the first that applies:

1. no `BuildInfo` for this project → `"missing-buildinfo"`
2. any output listed in the build info absent from `fileTimes` →
   `"missing-output"`
3. any input's time **greater than** `builtAt` → `"stale-input"`. An input
   absent from `fileTimes` also counts as stale — it was deleted, and the
   output no longer corresponds to reality.
4. any referenced project with no build info, or with a `builtAt` **greater
   than** this project's → `"stale-reference"`
5. otherwise → `"up-to-date"`

Note the strict comparisons. A file written in the same millisecond as the
build is *not* stale, which is deliberate — the alternative rebuilds forever on
filesystems with coarse timestamps.

Examples, with `snapshot.fileTimes = { "a.ts": 10, "out/a.js": 20 }` and
`buildInfos = { app: { outputs: ["out/a.js"], builtAt: 20 } }`:

- `statusOf({ path: "app", references: [] }, ["a.ts"], snapshot)` → `"up-to-date"`
- with `a.ts` at time `30` → `"stale-input"`
- with an extra output `"out/b.js"` that is not in `fileTimes` →
  `"missing-output"`

